title: Duplicate groups
tags: Duplicates
kind: reference
summary: Each set of identical files is a metarecord its members point at; its counters stay true as copies leave.

Each set of identical files is a ''metarecord of its own'', not a field repeated
on the files: a group can be queried, sorted and counted, and could one day carry
a decision of its own ("the copy I keep"). It is the same modelling as
hierarchical tags. It has no `mfr_path`: an abstract record, like a tag.

|!Field |!Type |!Meaning |
|`mf_schema` |string |`"duplicate_group"`: the metarecord's type |
|`mfr_content_hash` |string |the members' `mfr_full_hash` |
|`mfr_content_size` |int |the members' size, in bytes |
|`mfr_duplicate_count` |int |its members |
|`mfr_duplicate_reclaimable` |int |bytes freed by reducing the group to one file ([[What counts as a duplicate]]) |

Each member file carries `mfr_duplicate_group` (a `ref`) pointing at it.

A group is identified by the pair (size, hash), not the hash alone: members are
only ever compared within one size class, but two size classes could produce the
same hash, and the scan's find-or-create has to stay well defined. The count and
reclaimable space are redundant with the members and stored anyway, because
sorting is done on stored values: "the groups wasting the most space, worst
first" is one sorted query only if the number is a field.

!! The invariant

```
mfr_duplicate_group present  ⟹  the file exists and had an identical twin at the last scan
```

which makes `mfr_duplicate_group IS PRESENT` the exact query for "files having a
duplicate". Only the scan ever sets the link. It is cleared when the file's
content changes (with the hashes) and when the file goes away (`mfr_path` becomes
`Nothing` — the hashes are kept there, since relinking an orphan needs them).

!! Leaving a group

A member never leaves with a bare field clear: the group is ''recounted in the
same transaction'' — count and reclaimable space recomputed over the members that
remain, hard links included — and only what changed is written. A group left with
fewer than two members is ''dissolved'' at once: the group metarecord is deleted
and the survivor's link with it, since a file with no twin is not a duplicate.

That matters because these numbers are ''displayed'' — by `mf duplicate list` and
the [[duplicates panel]] — to someone deciding which copy to delete: "2 copies"
against a group that holds one invites a deletion that frees nothing and loses the
last copy.

A member leaves through the watcher (the file removed, or its content changed),
a trashing ([[What trashing does]]), `mf orphan clear`, and a rollback that
clears a path.

What still needs a scan is the other direction: a file that ''becomes'' a
duplicate. A group never grows between scans.
